From 3249eb4c3bc92ec84bec8a012a7b153f31bd658f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:55:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] enums: `#[repr(u128)]` / `#[repr(i128)]`, and the enum cast
 fold's 32-bit discriminants

repr128 is stable since Rust 1.89; mrustc rejected the attribute:

    error: Unknown enum repr 'u128'

Supporting it touches every stage that holds a discriminant:

- expand/AST/HIR: the u128/i128 reprs (HIR::Enum::Repr appended, so the
  serialised tags of the others are unchanged); discriminants are U128.
  Narrow reprs keep exactly the old value (the 64-bit pattern, negative
  values sign-extended to 64) and the 64-bit wrap of the implicit `+ 1`;
  u128/i128 keep all 128 bits.
- serialisation: the high words are written only for u128/i128 enums, so
  rlibs of existing crates are unchanged (an 0281-built rlib loads).
- layout: a 128-bit tag always uses the `Values` mode (also for data enums
  without explicit discriminants and for repr(C, u128)), and never serves
  as a niche: the `Linear`/niche paths treat a tag as a C scalar, and an
  emulated i128 is a struct.
- const eval: discriminant_value and the variant lookup from a literal
  read 128 bits; the lookup compares modulo the tag width (TypeRepr's
  new find_value, also used by get_enum_variant).
- codegen_c: a match on a 128-bit tag is an `if` chain (an emulated i128
  can't be switched on, a native `case` label holds 64 bits), tag values
  are emitted with emit_constant, `as` casts of an emulated tag copy both
  halves (128-bit targets) or take `.lo` (narrower ones). The `Values` tag
  paths now go through emit_enum_path, as the `Linear` ones did, so the
  leading tag of repr(C) data enums is `.TAG`, not `.DATA.TAG`.

Found on the way, a silent miscompile in existing builds: MIR constant
propagation folded `Variant as T` for a known variant through
HIR::Enum::get_value, which returns uint32_t. `Ordering::Less as i64`
(repr(i8), -1) folded to 4294967295, and a repr(u64) discriminant past
2^32 lost its high bits (the old binary: -1 -> 4294967295,
0x100000006 -> 0x6). The fold now takes the discriminant as a value of
the repr type (sign- or zero-extended) and truncates it like the integer
casts next to it.

Known difference from rustc: `Option<E>` of a repr(u128) enum gets its own
tag, while rustc uses the invalid tag values as a niche, so the sizes
differ.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
